Subject: [PATCH 16/29] =?UTF-8?q?mobile:=20key=20bar=20modifiers=20apply?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20to=20the=20next=20key=20once;=20=ED=95=9C/=EC=98=81,=20?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=ED=95=9C=EC=9E=90=20and=20a=20shortcut=20row?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The key bar's Ctrl, Alt, Shift and Win only flipped flags that applied to every
later key, Backspace and click until tapped off, so a forgotten Ctrl turned
typing (and the Backspaces of Korean syllable replacement) into shortcuts.
The first tap now arms a modifier for the next key or click only (filled), a
second tap keeps it on (outlined), a third turns it off. Shift+click and
Ctrl+click work and then release. Only the release of a press this session
sent uses a one-shot modifier up, so pinching or panning with two fingers to
aim (they end with a stray left release) keeps it armed. A modifier a key
event turned on keeps the old toggle-off. Leaving the session clears the key
bar state. A modifier tap or a shortcut first sends the text the keyboard
typed just before it, with the modifiers it was typed with.

With Ctrl, Alt or Win armed from the key bar, Hangul from the soft keyboard
is not sent: it is no key a shortcut can take, and when the keyboard joined
the jamo to the syllable before it (하 + ㄱ -> 학) the edit's first Backspace
went out as Ctrl+Backspace or Alt+Backspace. The edit is dropped, the field
starts over and the modifier stays armed for the next key, on every Korean
layout.

For Windows peers the bar gets 한/영 (VK_HANGUL) and 한자 (VK_HANJA), and a
⌘ toggle opens a row with Win, Win+D, Win+Tab, Alt+Tab, Alt+F4, Ctrl+Z/Y/A/X/F
and, when the peer allows it, Ctrl+Alt+Del. All keys use existing KEY_MAP
names; no protocol or host change.

(Research patch ios-keyboard B without its Korean-layout shortcut mapping,
plus the live-state and reset fixes from its verification and the plan
review's one-shot and Hangul fixes.)

